L000465	\subsubsection{Other periodograms}
L000466	\paragraph{$\ell$-1 periodogram}
L000467	The $\ell$-1 periodogram (\cite{Hara_2016}) is designed to detect periodic signals in unevenly sampled time series, similar to the Lomb-Scargle (\cite{Scargle_1982}) periodogram, but less prone to aliasing. Primarily developed for exoplanet detection in radial velocity data, it can also be applied to other fields requiring periodicity detection. We use this periodogram to analyze the split data and find identical results to those obtained from the GLS in Sect.~\ref{subsub:GLSchunk}, thus opting for not reporting it here for the sake of brevity.
L000468	
L000469	\paragraph{PDC, \texttt{USuRPER} and \texttt{SPARTA} periodograms}
L000470	The Phase Distance periodogram (PDC) (\cite{Shay_2017}) is a model-independent tool for periodicity detection in astronomical time series, designed to detect general periodicities in unevenly sampled data. \cite{Binnenfeld_2020} extended it via \texttt{USuRPER}, a method for detecting periodic variation in time series of astronomical spectra. The novelty of \texttt{USuRPER} lies in its ability to detect periodic variations in the overall spectra directly, without relying on specific scalar quantities like radial velocities or activity indicators. This method is ideal for identifying and analyzing signals associated with stellar activity and pulsations. \cite{Binnenfeld_2020} further enhanced this approach, enabling the separation of spectral shape variations from bulk Doppler shifts related to orbital motion.\\
L000471	
L000472	We used SPectroscopic vARiabiliTy Analysis (\texttt{SPARTA}) (\cite{Shahaf_2020}), a suite of tools that includes \texttt{USuRPER}, to apply these periodograms to all the three epochs H-08, C-16 and C-24 and found no signal at the SPI$\pm$ periods. The results from this analysis showed periodic signals at 2.24 and 1.79\,d  corresponding to non-Doppler spectral variations. These signals are not alias of each other and are probably not related with our SPI signals.\\
